# PART 3: THE MOST IMPORTANT CONCEPT — UNIQUE TOUR CODE

## 3.1 Every Confirmed Tour Must Have a Unique Tour Code

Every confirmed tour must receive a unique, permanent Tour Code.

Example:

`LST-2026-00001`

`LST-2026-00002`

`LST-2026-00003`

The format must be configurable.

The Tour Code becomes the central reference for every department.

Once an employee enters or selects a Tour Code, the system should retrieve all information associated with that tour.

This includes:

- Client or B2B agent.
- Actual traveller/customer information.
- Salesperson.
- Destination.
- Package name.
- Itinerary.
- Travel dates.
- Guest count.
- Room requirements.
- Quotation and revisions.
- Selling price.
- Customer advances.
- Customer outstanding amount.
- Supplier details.
- Hotel reservations.
- Transport arrangements.
- Vehicle allocation.
- Guide and activity bookings.
- Supplier advances.
- Supplier outstanding amounts.
- Internal expenses.
- Vouchers.
- Operational instructions.
- Destination-wise team assignments.
- Tour status.
- Actual expenses.
- Final profitability.

The Tour Code must be searchable throughout the entire application.

## 3.2 Different Identifiers for Different Business Stages

Do not use one identifier for everything.

Maintain separate references:

- Lead ID: First enquiry.
- Enquiry ID: Formal travel requirement.
- Quotation ID: Each quotation and its revisions.
- Booking ID: Confirmed booking record.
- Tour Code: Unique reference for the confirmed tour.
- Supplier Booking ID: Individual supplier reservation.
- Payment ID: Individual financial transaction.
- Expense ID: Individual expense record.
- Voucher ID: Individual generated travel document.

All these records must be linked correctly.

## 3.3 Tour Code Behaviour

The Tour Code must be generated only after the configured booking-confirmation conditions are satisfied.

Possible conditions:

- Client approval received.
- Booking confirmation recorded.
- Required advance received or an authorised credit/exception approved.
- Sales manager approval, if required.

Make these conditions configurable.

Once generated:

- The code must never change.
- It must not be reused.
- All departments must use the same code.
- Every related record must retain the reference.
- Revisions must not generate a duplicate Tour Code for the same confirmed tour.
- If a confirmed tour is cancelled, retain its history and code.
- If one booking is split into multiple independently operated tours, create linked child Tour Codes under the original booking or group reference.

